5. 404/not-found behaviours preserved by the sentinels         => PASS
   (with one adjacent behaviour change reported below for adjudication)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
  git show HEAD:internal/handlers/accounts/handlers.go > /tmp/.../handlers_orig.go
  grep -n 'StatusNotFound\|renderError' on both the original and the current file.

Original (HEAD)                              Current (merged)
  :265 "Account not found" StatusNotFound  ->  :275 "Account not found" StatusNotFound
  :317 "Anchor not found"  StatusNotFound  ->  :328 "Anchor not found"  StatusNotFound
  :141/:163 "Missing account id" 400       ->  :151/:173 identical
  :208 "Missing account id" 400            ->  :216 identical
  :167/:212 "Invalid form data: "+err 400  ->  :177/:220 identical
  :286 "Missing account or anchor id" 400  ->  :295 identical
  :291 "Invalid anchor date" 400           ->  :300 identical
Both 404 strings and both status codes are byte-identical; they are now reached
via errors.Is on errAccountNotFound (handlers.go:274) and errAnchorNotFound
(handlers.go:327), declared at handlers.go:41-49. On the MCP side
errAnchorAccountVanished (anchor.go:19-24) reproduces the original message
verbatim -- the fmt.Errorf string at anchor.go:238 is character-for-character
the string that was at anchor.go's pre-change `if !found` branch (confirmed in
`git diff HEAD -- internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/anchor.go`, where the same
literal appears on both a removed and an added line). Package tests for both
packages pass: `go test -race -count=1 ./internal/handlers/accounts/
./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/` -> ok / ok.

FINDING (advisory; outside criterion 5's literal text, but bears on the brief's
"Behaviour visible to users does not change"): three error paths that were HTTP
500 are now HTTP 200. In the original, an accounts.Load failure inside
handleDelete (HEAD:181), handleAddAnchor (HEAD:246) and handleDeleteAnchor
(HEAD:296) produced
    renderError(w, "Failed to load accounts: "+err.Error(), http.StatusInternalServerError)
Those three renderError calls are gone from the merged file (the current file
has no "Failed to load accounts" at all except the buildPageData one at :121).
A load failure inside Mutate now falls into the generic error branch --
handlers.go:198-203, :277-282, :330-335 -- which does
    data, _ := buildPageData(r); data.Error = err.Error(); renderList(w, r, data)
and renderList writes http.StatusOK (handlers.go:540-545). So on a corrupt or
unreadable accounts.json these three endpoints change from
"500 + Failed to load accounts: <err>" to "200 + <err> in the page body", and
the error string loses its prefix. applyForm similarly drops its
`fmt.Errorf("failed to load accounts: %w", ...)` wrapper (HEAD:405-408). This
is a real, evidenced observable change; it is not covered by any existing test,
and no criterion I was given names it. Recorded for the lead to adjudicate
against the brief's no-behaviour-change clause.

